1 OCCU 36 years as a social worker in Chicago, Illinois
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://worldconnect.rootsweb.ancestry.com/cgi-bin/igm.cgi?op=GET&db=rb9&id=I066168
2 CONT
2 CONT Deceased Name: Thomas F. McGee, social worker 
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas F. McGee, a former Chicago social worker who was an expert on A. Conan Doyle's famous detective Sherlock Holmes, died Tuesday in a Littleton, Colo., hospital.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. McGee, 66, had amyotrophic lateral sclerosis, better known as Lou Gehrig's disease.
2 CONT
2 CONT He retired in 1994 from his job as a supervising caseworker for the Illinois Department of Public Aid. He had been with the state agency for 36 years.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. McGee was an officer in Hugo's Companions and a member of the Hounds of Baskerville, the two oldest Chicago chapters of the international group the Baker Street Irregulars. About 10 years ago, he reached the highest honor acco
2 CONC rded a Sherlockian when he was "invested" in the international organization.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. McGee helped lead a fund-raising drive for a special headstone for the Graceland grave of Vincent Starrett, a founder of the Baker Street Irregulars.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. McGee received a degree in social work from the University of Washington in Seattle and did graduate work at the University of Chicago.
2 CONT
2 CONT He served as an Army cryptographer during the Korean conflict, then worked as a commercial artist in New York.
2 CONT
2 CONT Surviving are his wife, Bette; two daughters, Amy Osborne and Kathryn Nikjoo; a sister, Mary Dorsher; his mother, Ruth, and two granddaughters.
2 CONT
2 CONT Burial will be private. An August memorial service is being planned for Chicago.
