2 CONT View all 2 photos
2 CONT
2 CONT Balliett, Col. Howard D.
2 CONT
2 CONT Col Howard D. Balliett (Dad) was a military officer, athlete, diplomat, musician, and educator. Born in August, 1918 in Augusta, Wisconsin, he worked his way through Ripon College as a pianist for churches and on the radio station
2 CONC , graduating in 1939 and capturing the S.N. Pickard award for best scholar-athlete plus national honors as a Little All-American football player. He entered the Army as an infantry officer after graduation, stationed in Hawaii, an
2 CONC d played on the Army basketball team. And when his college sweetheart (nee Marilynn M. Bruns) came to Hawaii, they were married in August, 1941. Their home was strafed four months later by Japanese fighters in the Pearl Harbor att
2 CONC ack, and while Mom volunteered with the Red Cross, Dad dug in his unit on the beach to repel the expected invasion.
2 CONT
2 CONT Dad organized and set up a Ranger training camp in New Guinea, and its graduates conducted raids in the Pacific Theater throughout WWII. After the war, an assignment as Professor of Military Science at Wofford College in South Car
2 CONC olina was followed by diplomatic postings with Military Assistance Advisory Groups (MAAG) in London, Germany, and Japan. His two older sons (Jeff and Tim) played Little League baseball, and in Japan Dad coached them (twice) to the
2 CONC  All-Japan Little League World SeriesChampionship, beating the other 24 military bases deployed there. His youngest son, Tom, joined the family in Germany.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sprinkled along the way were infantry assignments with troop units at Fort Hood, Texas and Ft Riley, Kansas. These were the assignments he liked best. The camaraderie of his cohesive units was legendary, as were the soldier's unit
2 CONC  training scores and the parties that followed… with his piano and lyrics written for the occasion at the center.Dad also managed to win post championships in handball and tennis at every post where he served. As Regimental Comman
2 CONC der of the 31st Infantry (Bearcats), Dad was assigned a cadre, which then had to recruit its own soldiers, train them, and deploy them to Korea.
2 CONT
2 CONT Later in his career he was assigned to the Office of Legislative Liaison to coordinate the Army's affairs with Congress, and then to the Deputy Chief of Staff for the Supreme Headquarters Allied Powers Europe (SHAPE) in Versailles
2 CONC , France. Dad extended his education in the process with a Master's degree in International Relations from George Washington University.Upon his retirement in 1969 (then Deputy Post Commander of Fort Riley), he returned to Wiscons
2 CONC in with the Wisconsin State University at Oshkosh, rising to Vice Chancellor over the next thirteen years.His natural tendency for leadership followed him to retirement in Sarasota, and he became the President of the Golf Associat
2 CONC ion at Palm-Aire. In keeping with his athletic history, he won (with Tim) the club member guest tournament (twice).
2 CONT
2 CONT Jeff passed in 1984, and Marilynn, his wife of 64 years, preceded him in 2005. He is survived by his sister, Sheila Rahl (Wisconsin), his sons Tim (Leesburg, FL) and Tom (Tucson, Arizona), five grandchildren, and eight great grand
2 CONC -children. He was 96 years old.
2 CONT
2 CONT In lieu of flowers, donations can be provided to The Jeffrey Dayle Balliett Scholarship Fund at 300 Seward Street, Ripon College, Ripon, Wisconsin, 54971. - See more at: http://www.legacy.com/obituaries/thenorthwestern/obituary.as
