1 NOTE He was chr. Oct 10, 1795 on Eidal According to family legend, Timand Olsen Eidal, born 1795, was the original recipient of Kvarve, one of the four remnants after the quadripartite division of the Eidal Farm near Nes, Norway, the o
2 CONC ther three remnants of which were: Nordgaarden, Sydgaarden, and Swengaarden.  The name KVARVE presumably means "bend in the river", which refers to the location of the original Kvarve Farm.  As it is Norwegian custom to refer to t
2 CONC hose living on a particular farm by the farm name, TIMAN  acquired the name KVARVE when he took up residence on the Kvarve Farm, and consequently he was the oldest ancestor to bear the name and is in this sense the progenitor of t
2 CONC he genealogy.
2 CONT
2 CONT Pg. 170 of Gerhard Naeseth's book for 1847-48.  Timan and Kirsti and a number of their relatives emigrated from Drammen, Norway to America in 1848 on the barque Drafna, which  arrived in New York on August 4, 1848 from Drammen, wi
2 CONC th 157 passengers including 57 children and 7 babies. Captain H. E. Eckersberg.
2 CONT
2 CONT Timan died enroute and was buried in Brooklyn, NY.  The immigration party continued north from New York, New York up the Hudson River to Albany, NY, over the New York State Barge Canal from Albany to Buffalo, NW; Southwest over La
2 CONC ke Erie;  South on Lake Michigan to Muskegon, WI; and by land from there to Rock Prairie, Rock County, WI where they established the Quarve Farm.
2 CONT
2 CONT Pg. 173 - #1023 - Tidemand Olsen Quarve [Tideman O. Quarve, 52, m], son of Ole Pedersen Eidal and Kari Knudsdtr. baptized October 10, 1795, Emigrated from Nes, Buskerud, died in New York, 1848.  He was married Jun 13, 1820 to Kjer
2 CONC sit Jørgensdtr. Garnaas [Kristi Quarve, 56, f]
