2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT Rites Held for Peder Pladson Well Known Resident of Hatton Died at Northwood Last Friday, Dec. 13
2 CONT
2 CONT Funeral services were held Wednesday afternoon at 2 o’clock from Goose River Church for Peder Pladson, pioneer resident of this community, who passed away last Friday [Dec. 13] at the Old People’s Home at Northwood following a str
2 CONC oke. Rev. K. C. Grundahl officiated at the rites. Burial was in the churchyard cemetery.
2 CONT
2 CONT Peder Pladson was born in Houston County, Minn. Oct. 11, 1861.   At the age of two years, he moved with his parents, Knut and Barbo Pladson to Northwood, Iowa, where he grew to manhood.   Later, he moved to Newburgh Township near 
2 CONC Hatton, where he homesteaded and which was his farm until he retired.   He moved into the city in 1936 and lived here until last July when he and Mrs. Pladson moved to the Old People’s Home at Northwood. Mr. Pladson had suffered s
2 CONC everal strokes and had been in ill health for some time.
2 CONT
2 CONT As one of the early pioneers, Mr. Pladson was also one of the charter members of the Goose River Lutheran Church.
2 CONT
2 CONT He leaves to mourn, his wife, Ingeborg [Noss, third wife], and the following children, Alfred, at home; Carl of Winnipeg; Edgar of Minneapolis; Oliver of Hatton; Gilbert of Finley; Alice (Mrs. Roy Flaten) of Crookston; Esther (Mrs
2 CONC . Pabst) of St. Paul; Ann (Mrs. Alexander Jenson) of Minneapolis; and May (Mrs. Ed Von Rueden) of Grand Forks. There are also 18 grandchildren and great-grandchildren. Three children preceded him in death. – Hatton Free Press, Dec
2 CONC . 19, 1946
