1 NOTE From the Richwood Township History, near Detroit, MN.  --A streak of sandy soil in what is now the city of Detroit Lakes led to the first staking of claims in Richwood Township.  It was May 27, 1870, that the Hans Hanson and Iver 
2 CONC Christensen families left Spring Grove, Minn., with the intention of going northwest where they could take up land under the homestead laws.  On July 3, 1870, they reached Floyd Lake and from there continued to Oak Lake.  There th
2 CONC ey left their families and drove west for about five miles to pick out their claims.  They were told, however, that it was useless to look for land as the land hunters were already quarreling amongst themselves over their claims. 
2 CONC So they returned to Detroit Lake on the same day.  The next morning was July 4th and the pioneers decided to observe the National Holiday by relaxing and not working.  According to an account written later by Hanson, "On July 5th 
2 CONC we commenced to break land along the foot of what afterwards was called Fox's Hill, which is just back of the present Hotel Minnesota..  "After making a few rounds we came to places where the ground was rather sandy. Mr. Christens
2 CONC en said the ground was not good for much.  "I agreed with him, but said that if ever the railroad came through, they would be almost sure to get a small town on that prairie.  "To my remark, Mr. Christensen said he was not looking
2 CONC  for a townsite, but for land that would make a good farm.  "We then drove our oxen with the breaking plough back to the wagons where our families were and told our wives that we had to pack up our things again and leave.  "...On 
2 CONC the 6th day of July we started out again and went west as far as Oak Lake, where we left our families.  We then went north on the White Earth trail.  Another man by the name of Iver Iverson had then joined us.  "When we went north
2 CONC  about five miles from Oak Lake, we found some nice oak groves and good prairie lands right up to the timber and this suited us.  "The land was so rich that the grass reached nearly up to our arms on the highest parts of the prair
2 CONC ie.  We all picked out adjoining claims that day."
2 CONT
2 CONT The three men were warned, a week after their arrival, that the land alongside the timber had already been claimed by people from White Earth and about 70 of them had organized into a combination to drive away any person who would
2 CONC  try to take their claims.  The men oiled their rifles and stated that they were determined not to move again. A few days later they were told by Dr. Pyle, government Indian doctor, that they were safe.
2 CONT
2 CONT In relating the troubles caused by the grasshopper scourge, Hanson wrote:  "They destroyed nearly everything that came before them, even our clothes, if they could get at them.  They stayed with us for about seven years, and destr
2 CONC oyed almost everything that we planted every year. Potatoes and vegetables were nearly all destroyed.  It looked rather blue those years!"  On June 23, 1871, the township of Richwood was organized and the first town meeting was he
2 CONC ld in Haney's sawmill on Sept. 29, 1871.  The first town officers were W. W. McLeod, chairman, Ole Qualley and Sivert Sampson, supervisors; Hans Hanson, clerk; Gabriel Halvorson, treasurer and Iver Christensen and John Anderson, j
2 CONC ustices.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hans was married to another lady in Spring Grove with whom he had two children, one a boy.  They moved to Locust Lane, Iowa where his wife died.  Later he returned to Spring Grove where he married Miss Sigrid Myhro.  The family mo
2 CONC ved to Richwood township in 1870 and lived six years, then moving to Detroit where they have since lived.  Hans served twelve terms as register of deeds, he was county coroner for ten years and a member of the village council of D
2 CONC etroit.  Hans and his family lived in Richwood Township for six years and then moved to Detroit.
2 CONT
2 CONT Detroit Record August 16, 1912 - FINAL CALL COMES SUDDENLY - Pioneer Citizen and Ex-County Official Dies of Heart Ddisease While Eating Breakfast.  -- The uncertainty of life was again effectually demonstrated at the Hans Hanson h
2 CONC ome Wednesday morning when Mr. Hanson was stricken with apoplexy while eating his breakfast, death ensuing immediately. While deceased had been ailing for the past few years, yet he was able to be about the house and his demise wa
2 CONC s as unexpected as it was sudden.
2 CONT Hans Hanson has been among the pioneer leaders in the upbuilding of Becker County, for here he has resided for forty-two years.  On July 7th, 1870, Mr. Hanson together with Iver Christianson and their respective famiies, located o
2 CONC n claims in Richwood township, then a prairie wildesrness.  Here they toiled and made their homes, Mr. Hanson becoming the first clerk of the township upon its organization.  On January 6th, 1878, he was elected register of deeds 
2 CONC of the county and served until January 5, 1897, being defeated by O. N. Nohen of Atlanta.  The following campaign Mr. Hanson was again a candidate for the office, was re-elected, took office on January 3, 1899 and served until Jan
2 CONC uary 6, 1903.  He made an ideal official and held the respect of every man with whom he was brought in contact.
2 CONT Hans Hanson was born in Hallingdal, Norway on November 27, 1842, and came to this country with his parents when he was ten years of age. The family first settled in Wisconsin but later moved to Spring Grove, MN where he was first 
2 CONC married.  Two children were born of this union, one of whom died and the other, a son, is serving as a soldier in the Phillippine Islands.  Mr. Hanson then moved to Locast Lane, Iowa where his wife died.  Later he returned to Spri
2 CONC ng Grove, MN where he was united in marriage to Miss Sigrid Myhro.
2 CONT The family came to Richwood Township as stated in 1870 where they resided for six years, then moving to Detroit where they have since lived.  In addition to serving twelve terms as register of deeds, Mr. Hanson was also county cor
2 CONC oner for ten years and a member of the village coucil of Detroit.  Nine children have been born to the second marriage, three of whom are dead.  The others, whom, with their mother are still living are as follows:  Mrs. H. O. Nord
2 CONC by, Gary, MN; Mrs. Sam Anderson, Richwood; Ole Hanson, Detroit; Mrs. L. A. Berg, Detroit; Mrs. John Ekern, Detroit; Edwin Hanson, Canada.  The funeral is to be held tomorrow at 2:30 in the afternoon from the Norwegian Synod church
2 CONC  with services conducted by the pastor, Rev. A. G. Quammen and interment will be made in Oak Grove cemetery.  This paper joins with the hundreds of friends throughout the county in extending condolences to the bereaved family duri
2 CONC ng this, their hour of affliction.
1 NOTE Witnesses at Maria's and Han's wedding were: Engebret Hansen Melbraaten and Henrik Nielsen Tangen.
1 NOTE
2 CONT Witnesses for their wedding were:  Ingebright Hansen Melbraaten and Ole Arnesen Myhro
