1 NOTE
2 CONT Pg. 173 - Gerhard Naeseth's Book on Immigration 1848 - #1026 - Jørgen Tidemandsen Quarve [Jorgen T. Quarve, 22, m], born November 3, 1826. Emigrated with his mother to the Luther Valley settlement, Rock CO., WI; 1853 to Houston Co
2 CONC ., MN. Used the name George Timanson Quarve in America.  Member, 1859 MN Legislature.  Died January 17, 1878. Married, January 2, 1852, Liv Knudsdtr Aasen (1848:1859), born September 16, 1824.  She had emigrated from Nes, Buskerud
2 CONC  in 1848. Jørgen is said to have married a second time, Ingeborg Halvorsdtr. Persmoen, born February 2, 1836.  Jørgen was married a third time, Olaug.  Fourteen children, including:  Kjersti, born October 19, 1852.
2 CONT
2 CONT Both Jørgen and Liv Knudsdtr., his first wife came on the boat called the "Drafna" and arrived in New York on August 7, 1848 from Drammen, with 157 passengers including 57 children and 7 babies.  Captain H. E. Eckersberg.
2 CONT
2 CONT Came to Spring Grove township in 1853.
2 CONT
2 CONT Koren's Journal-   1854 - October 19th after Trinity.  Worship Services at Jorgen Quarve.  Eleven children baptized and churchings, and forty-seven communicants.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage License No 159 at District Court of Houston County - Know Ye, That license is hereby granted to join together as husband and wife, George Temanson age, 40, of the County of Houston State of Minnesota and Ingborg Halvorsdt
2 CONC r, age 30, of the County of Houston, State of Minnesota after being satisfied by the oath of George Temanson, the party applying for the license.  Dated at Caledonia, MN Feb 5, 1867, John Dorsh
2 CONT
2 CONT Pg. 481 - History of Houston County 1882 - George Timanson, deceased, was one of the pioneers of this place and a prominent politician.  He was born in Norway on the 14th of November 1826 and came to America, locating in Wisconsin
2 CONC  in 1848.  He came to Spring Grove in 1853 and immediately commenced making improvements on the land in section fifteen.  His house being one of the largest of the first settlers, it became the place for holding religious services
2 CONC , Mr. Timanson taking an active interest in the organization and maintenance of the church. Besides filling many local offices he was a member of the State Legislature from this district.  His death occurred on the 17th of January
2 CONC  1878.  He was the father of fourteen children, nine of whom are now living.
