2 CONT Pg. 173 - Gerhard Naeseth's book on Immigration to the United States 1847-48 - #1027 - Levor Tidemandsen Quarve [Levor T. Quarve, 18 m] born December 27, 1830, died December 24, 1920.  Moved 1853 to near Spring Grove, MN.  Married
2 CONC  May 20, 1856, Kristi Knudsdtr. Berg, daughter of Knud Eriksen Berg, born May 3, 1835.  He had emigrated from Nes, Buskerud in 1850.  Eleven children including:  Caroline; Timan, born March 17, 1857, died November 13, 1922; Knut b
2 CONC orn November 22, 1858; Olave; Ella and Edward.  Levor came on the ship "Drafna" in 1848.  It arrived in New York on August 7, 1848 from Drammen with 157 passengers including 57 children and 7 babies.  Captain H. E. Eckersberg.
2 CONT
2 CONT Came to Spring Grove township in 1853.
2 CONT
2 CONT Pg. 481 History of Houston County 1882 - Levor Timanson Quarve, a son of Thideman Olson Eidahl and Kjersti Jorgensen Garness was born the 27th of December, 1830 in the parish of Naes, Hallingdal, Norway.  He was reared on a farm, 
2 CONC spending most of his time herding cattle in the mountains. His father had a large family of children, whom he endeavored to bring up practical men and women, but in those mountainous districts their school advantages were very lim
2 CONC ited.  The instructions imparted to Levor were at home in a catechism, his school days being but three, writing and arithmetic being learned after he attained manhood.  In 1848 the family emigrated to America.  The journey was lon
2 CONC g and wearisome, attended by many hardships.  The father was removed by death soon after landing in New York, and a month later the family came to Rock County, WI where they were welcomed by a few countrymen who had come the year 
2 CONC before.  Levor found employment as a carpenter, mason and farm laborer.  In 1853, in company with some friends, he  made a trip to Southern Minnesota and Iowa in search of good farming land.  The following spring Mr. Timanson loca
2 CONC ted two miles west of Spring Grove where he still resides engaged in farming and stock raising.  Having thus procured himself a home he married Miss Kresti Knudsen Berg in May, 1856.  The result of this union was eleven children, 
2 CONC seven of whom are living.  Mr. Timanson now owns land to the amount of eight hundred and forty acres, five hundred and fifty of which are under cultivation.  He has taken a prominent part in building and supporting schools and chu
2 CONC rches and during the war contributed largely and spent a large amount of time in obtaining volunteers.  He is an exemplary husband and father, and bore his full share of trials during his pioneer life.  For a time he stood alone i
2 CONC n politics, being the only republican in the town.  His mother died in her ninety-first years in the fall of 1881 at the residence of her son, Levor.  At the time of her husband's death, while in a foreign among entire strangers, 
2 CONC she was driven nearly to despair, but bore her lot patiently, placing her trust in the Guardian of the widow and the fatherless.  Before her death her faith was amply rewarded by seeing prosperity smile upon the homes of her child
2 CONC ren and children's children.
2 CONT
2 CONT StorlieDelaneyRobinson070722 209 (21)
