1 NOTE
2 CONT Pg 172 - Gerhard Naeseth's book Emigration to America 1847-48 - #1018 - Niels Olsen Onsgaard [Niels O. Onsgaard, 11, m] born May 21, 1837, died August 13, 1914.  Moved to Spring Grove, MN, where he was a stone mason, sold general 
2 CONC merchandise and farm implements.  Established the Onsgard State Bank of which he was president until his death. Married, July 1864, Guri Olsdtr Trostem (1848:1007), daughter of Ole Guulsen Trostem and Kari Olsdtr., born February 9
2 CONC , 1847, died 1923. She had emigrated from Nes, Buskerud in 1848.  Children include: Barbro, born 1864, died 1882; Kari, born 1866, died 1954, Oline, born 1872, died 1963, Nellie, born 1877, died 1972; Oliver, born 1882, died 1939;
2 CONC  Baldwin, born 1887, died 1960.  He and his parents came on the ship "Drafna" which arrived in New York on August 7, 1848 from Drammen with 157 passengers including 57 children and 7 babies.  Captain H. E. Eckersberg.
2 CONT
2 CONT Nels Olsen Onsgard, one of the pioneer business men of the village and well and favorably known thruout the entire county died at his home in Spring Grove.  He had been ailing in health for the last year but was not confined to hi
2 CONC s room for more than a week when old age and uremia ended his long and useful business career.  He was buried on August 16, 1914 at the age of 77 years.
2 CONT
2 CONT At the age of 12 years he came to America in company with his parents and settled at Orfordville, WI.  After his marriage in 1864, they came to Spring Grove where they have since resided and during the latter years have had one of
2 CONC  the finest homes in our little city.  He was a stone mason by trade when he came here and was engaged in this work for several years after which he opened a general merchandise establishment in company with the late Nels Hendrick
2 CONC son.  In 1870, he sold his interest and for a time was engaged in the sale of farm implements.  In 1879, he formed a partnership with H. E. Kieland and they conducted a general store for many years.  In the 1890's he established a
2 CONC  private bank and conducted same until 1907 when it was incorporated into a State Bank.  He was president of this bank until his death.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. Onsgard was a quiet, industrious and economical man with a keen eye for the public welfare.  He held many offices of public trust, such as postmaster for over twenty five years, town clerk for many years and was village treasu
2 CONC rer for years and years.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was a man of generous impulses and never forgot the hospitable ways of the pioneer.  He had borne adversity bravely and enjoyed properity quietly.  He had filled the various relations of life, as son, husband, father, brother, 
2 CONC friend and filled them well.  Who can do more?
2 CONT
2 CONT But he is gone.  Another name stricken from the ever lessening roll of our old settlers and a solitary woman in the sunset of life and a lonely home, are left to attest how sadly they will miss him.  It must be so; these tender hu
2 CONC man ties cannot be severed without a pang.  Yet in such a death there is really no cause for grief.  His life work was done and well done.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was a man who united sound sense with strong convictions, and a candid outspoken temper, eminently fitted to mould the rude elements of pioneer society into form and consistency and aid in raising a high standard of citizenship
2 CONC  in our midst.  How much this community owes him and such as he, is impossible to estimatae, though it would be a grateful task to trace his influence through some of the more direct channels and to hld him up in these degenerate 
2 CONC days in his various characters of husband and father of neighbor and friend.  But it comes not within the scope of this brief article to do so.  Suffice to say, he lived nobly and died peacefully at the advance age of 77 years. Mr
2 CONC . and Mrs. Onsgard celebrated their Golden Wedding anniversary at their home July 17, 1914.
2 CONT
2 CONT Jul 11, 1895 - The Vincent lot occupied by M. K. Storlie & Co's lumber yard was sold last week to Banker Onsgard for $1000.  This tract was purchased by Mr. Vincent a few years ago at a very nominal sum and has risen in value with
2 CONC out material improvements to the above figure.
2 CONT
2 CONT January 7, 1897 - N. O. Onsgard, Banker.  Domestic and Foreign Exchange. Tickets to and from all European points at lowest rates Via American, Allan, Thingvalla and various other steamship lines.
2 CONT
2 CONT August 8, 1901 - N. O. Onsgard has commenced excavating for his new bank building.  It will front on Wilmington Street. (this is the bank that was built in the City Park and torn down again.)
2 CONT
2 CONT January 1, 1903 - Domestic & Foreign Exchange. Tickets to and from all European points at lowest rates Via American, Allan, Thingvalla and various othe Steamship lines.
2 CONT
2 CONT Jun 16, 1904 - The Cement sidewalk builders are here.-- On Tuesday occurred the first move made by Spring Grove for the improvement of our sidewalks when a cement walk was placed around the dwelling of Banker Onsgard.  Brown Ander
2 CONC son, the next door neighbor, was the next party to take up the good move by handing in his order for both an outside and inside walk.  He has been bothered considerable with the water running from the street through his yard every
2 CONC  spring and expects to remedy it by building a cement walk.  A great many seem to be interested now since the improvement has commenced and if present indications continue there will be a big supply of these walks in town before l
2 CONC ong.  Let it boom.  We have some of the nicest dwelling properties in the county and with the addition of cement walks, they will be made very attractive.
2 CONT
2 CONT February 22, 1906 - Capital - $25,000.00 - Established 1890 - 3 per cent interest paid on time deposits.  Safety Deposit Boxes to Rent. Writes Fire and Windstorm Insurance, Collects Taxes. Foreign Exchange Sold. Bank Money Orders 
2 CONC issued.  Money Loaned on Good Security.  N. O. Onsgard, Banker; O. N. Onsgard, Cashier; B. N. Onsgard, Ass't Cashier
2 CONT
2 CONT LUMBER YARDS
2 CONT
2 CONT 1919 History of Houston County: Martin K. Storlie one of the proprietors of the Spring Grove Lumber Company, a well-known and flourishing business concern, was born on a farm in sections 9 and 10, Spring Grove Township, this count
2 CONC y, March 13, 1870, son of Knute and Anna (Skorgen) Storlie. He acquired his elementary education in district school #55 and later attended Breckenridge Institute at Decorah, Iowa, completing a three years' course in 1888.  After t
2 CONC hat until 1891, he was engaged as a farm laborer and then went to Revillo, South Dakota where he was employed for a year and a half as clerk by William Storlie.  In 1893 he returned to Spring Grove and in that fall entered the gen
2 CONC eral store of Asle Halverson as clerk, being thus employed until the following year.  Then buying the interest of Lars Budahl in his lumber business in Spring Grove, he became associated as partner with Narve Narveson, the firm co
2 CONC nducting business under the style of Storlie & Narveson.  In 1896 Mr. Narveson sold his interest to Olson & Newhouse of Mabel, Minnesota and the firm became that of Storlie, Olson & Newhouse, which it remained until 1896.  Mr. New
2 CONC house then sold his interest to Nils Onsgard, a banker of Spring Grove, and the style of the firm was changed to Onsgard & Storlie, the two proprietors conducting the business until 1912, when, on the admission of Gerhard Roverud 
2 CONC as a third partner, the present Spring Grove Lumber Company was formed and has since remained unchanged as to name.  In the same year that the company was formed the C. L. Coleman yard was purchased and the two concerns consolidat
2 CONC ed.  In 1914, Mr. Onsgard died and his interest was taken over by his widow, who remains as one of the company, each partner owing a one-third interest, with Mr. Storlie as the acting manager.  The concern is one of the substantia
2 CONC l business enterprises of Spring Grove and as one of its owners, Mr. Storlie is numbered among the prosperous citizens of the village, where he owns a comfortable one-and-a-half story residence of seven rooms.  He also owns 150 ac
2 CONC res of land just on the edge of the village, as well as 160 acres near Balta, ND.  Though ostentatious, he is a good businessman and takes a keen interest in the prosperity and advancement of the community in which he resides.  In
2 CONC  politics he is a Republican.
1 CHAN
2 DATE 30 NOV 2014
3 TIME 06:58:58
