1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://digitalarkivet.arkivverket.no/ft/person/pf01038086003418
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Birth 104 https://media.digitalarkivet.no/view/1110/7098/235
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/119428848/gunhilde-a-myhre
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT from The Vindicator and Republican, Estherville, Emmet County, Iowa, March 28, 1917, posted by the Emmet County IAGenWeb Coordinator 2/25/2009, contributed by Ruth M. Hackett
2 CONT
2 CONT The Passing of a Pioneer
2 CONT
2 CONT Mrs. Ole Myhre, one of the pioneer settlers of Ellsworth township [Center township], passed to her reward Saturday morning, March 24th [1917], aged 61 years, 3 months and 21 days. The immediate cause of her death was pneumonia, ag
2 CONC gravated with chronic Bright's disease.
2 CONT
2 CONT Funeral was held from the Norwegian Lutheran church Tuesday afternoon, a very large concourse of relatives and friends being present with floral offerings to pay their last tribute to the departed.
2 CONT
2 CONT Miss Gurild [Gunnild] A. Brunsvold was born in Naes, Hallingdal, Norway July 20 1855. [Parents were Asle Brunsvold and Sigrid Herbranson according to Emmet County Death Records.] She came to America in 1876, locating at Kenyon, Mi
2 CONC nnesota. At this place she was united in marriage to Mr. Ole Myhre and she left with her husband for Emmet County, Iowa, where they arrived March 29th and settled on a homestead in Ellsworth township [Center township] where she li
2 CONC ved when called by Death.
2 CONT
2 CONT She was always a kind and loving mother as well as a devoted wife. She endured the trials and tribulations incident to pioneer life with great patience and fortitude. Uppermost in her mind was the great ideal of a fine family whic
2 CONC h she lived to see fulfilled, by watching grow to manhood and womanhood four splendid sons and four charming daughters.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mrs. Myhre was raised and educated in the Lutheran faith and reared her family in the same belief.
2 CONT
2 CONT Until March 18th deceased had been in her usual health, but at that time had an attack of bleeding at the nose which was found difficult to stop. This was followed by several other attacks and later pneumonia developed which was m
2 CONC ore than the weakened body could stand, and death came as a relief to her sufferings. All member of the family except one son, Lewis, who was unable to reach home on account of the blockade, were at the bedside and each received a
2 CONC  parting from their mother, who was conscious until the last. She clung to and caressed the hand of her husband and as the veil was slowing drawn hoped that she would meet them all in Heaven.
2 CONT
2 CONT Eleven children have been born to this union, seven boys and four girls of which eight survive her, as follows: Mrs. Sam Wicks, Estherville, Iowa; Mrs. E. H. Egertson, Wallingford; Alfred, Olaf and Mrs. William R. Anderson at Esth
2 CONC erville, Lewis of Sauk Center, Minnesota, Oscar amd Gudrun who are at home; and her husband. Eight grandchildren number the second generation. She leaves two sisters and one brother in America; Mrs. C. Lein at Jackson, Minnesota; 
2 CONC Mrs. O. B. Opheim, Northfield, Minnesota; and Mr. Sam Brunsvold, Petersburg, Minnesota. She also leaves one sister and two brothers who reside in Norway.
2 CONT
2 CONT The following acted as pall-bearers: Mr. Oliver Refsell, Mr. Nels Anderson, Mr. Lars Jacobson, Mr. Carl Anderson, Mr. Albert Romsa, and Mr. Martin Anderson.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/119428848/gunhilde-a-myhre
