1 NOTE After Teman's father's death, his mother married Halgrim Trulsen Frosager and when Teman was 18 yrs old, the family emigrated to America, settling at Rock Prairie, WIsconsin.  In 1854, they came to Spring Grove township settling o
2 CONC n a farm two miles south of the village.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. Frosager was of a forceful character and seen became a prominent member of the community.  Young Teman was reared on the farm and early trained to habits of industry, attending school but two weeks in this country.  When he ma
2 CONC rried Annie Kjos, the ceremony took place in the log cabin built by his stepfather.  The bride's sole effects consisted of a few articles tied up in a small bundle and the clothes she stood up in.
2 CONT
2 CONT On buying his stepfather's farm he began to cultivate, making use of oxen for draft animals.  During the seventeen years that elapsed until their removal to the village in 1874, he erected a number of buildings on the farm inclusi
2 CONC ng a substantial log house and a stave barn. Brownsville, 30 miles distant was his market place from which he hauled all the material for the buildings on the farm as well as for the hotel he built in the village in 1879.  This wa
2 CONC s a two-story brick building and was a stopping place for the traveling public for many years.
2 CONT
2 CONT Timan served as supervisor of Spring Grove township for a number of years and in 1871 was elected to represent his district in the State Legislature, serving during the session of 1871-72 under the Administration of Governor Austi
2 CONC n.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was a prominent member of the Norwegian Evangelical Lutheran church. In 1882, he had become the owner of 500 acres of land and later gave a 200 acre farm to each of his three sons and an 160 acre farm to two of his daughters.
2 CONT
2 CONT Their later years were spent in Parkland, Washington, Horace, North Dakota and Davenport , North Dakota.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hinkley House
2 CONT
2 CONT William Hinkley in 1855, or thereabouts, bought from Mr. James Smith his stock of goods from his store in the east part of Spring Grove. Mr. Hinkley erected a store about a half mile west of Smith's store and moved the stock of go
2 CONC ods into this building.  William Hinkley had purchased the land from Embrick Knudsen Opheim.  In addition to groceries and general merchandise, Mr. Hinkley was a dealer in wines and liquors.  In 1862 Mr. Mons Fladager was appointe
2 CONC d deputy postmaster and he kept it in his store for about a year, when Mr. Prentiss succeeded to the position and removed the post office to his hotel, the old Hinkley Store.
2 CONT Mr. Hinkley sold his store to Teman Gilbertson and Mr. Gilbertson kept a public house for several years, but in 1879 he erected a large brick house which was used as a hotel and stood on what is now the Spring Grove Public School 
2 CONC ground.  Mr. Gilbertson, after he built his brick hotel, used this building as a store room and some woodworking was also done here.
2 CONT
2 CONT February 5, 1903--Teman Gilbertson is getting ready to plat his land in the western portion of the village.  This land will offer many beautiful dwelling sites.
2 CONT
2 CONT April 21, 1904- The T. Gilbertson barn, the landmark of the west part of town, is being torn down.  Gust Gilbertson having secured it, is taking it apart for the purpose of moving it to his farm south of town.  (Where Harold Oleru
2 CONC d lived)
2 CONT
2 CONT August 11, 1904 - Julius Rollie and family moved into Mrs. Quinnell's residence south of town last week.  Carl Solie and family, who formerly occupied the place, are now comfortably domiciled in the Teman Gilbertson residence.
