1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Longtime Alaska resident Ned Syversrud, 98, died Oct. 29 at the Anchorage Pioneer Home. A service was at Witzleben's Bragaw Chapel, with Mark Huddle and Dan Lawty officiating. A visitation was one hour prior to the
2 CONC  service.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was born June 27, 1896, in Nesbyeu, Norway, to Knut and Andrea Syversrud. The family sailed to the United States when he was 6 months old and lived in Red Lake County, Minn. He moved to Hibbing, Minn., in 1922 when the iron ore
2 CONC  mines were started. Mr. Syversrud recalled that people said he was so tall he should become a policeman. And he did, working as a law officer until 1945 when he went to work on the Alaska Highway. He moved to Seattle with his fam
2 CONC ily in 1946 and worked for Boeing Co. as a carpenter for 20 years. He adopted Alaska as his home after his wife, Ingeborg, died in 1970. He was known at the Anchorage Senior Center for his beaded baskets. His family said, "He was 
2 CONC always busy making things with his hands."
2 CONT
2 CONT He is survived by his sons, Erling of California and Kenneth of Anchorage; his daughter, Dorothy Hardesty of Anchorage; his sisters, Gladys Borgen of Thief River Falls, Minn., and Ruth Mosbeck of Red Lake Falls, Minn.; four grandc
2 CONC hildren and four great-grandchildren. Burial was in the Washington Memorial Park in Seattle, Wash. 
