1 NOTE {geni:about_me} === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT Please post this obituary for Alfred P. Pladson on his Find A Grave site: Rites Friday for Alfred P. Pladson.    Funeral services will be held Friday afternoon, Feb. 26 at 2 o’clock in the Goose River Lutheran Church for Alfred P.
2 CONC  Pladson, longtime resident of this community, who passed away Monday [Feb. 22].    Rev. A.E. Setness will officiate and burial will be in the churchyard cemetery.
2 CONT
2 CONT Active pallbearers will be Carl Gulson, Elmer Engebretson, G.O. Thompson, Henry Huset, Arvid Hanson and Oliver Bjerke.   Honorary pallbearers will be Melvin Thompson, J.L. Thompson, Hjalmer Pederson, Carl Johnson, George Mehus and
2 CONC  Edwin O. Thompson.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. Pladson died at the Northwood hospital early Monday morning,  where he had been a patient for about ten days.   He had not been in the best of health for some time, but had been up and around previous to being hospitalized.   
2 CONC  Alfred P. Pladson was born in Newburgh Township, Steele County,  April 7, 1887, of parents, Mr. and Mrs. P.K. Pladson, pioneer residents of this area.   He made his home here until 1925 when he homesteaded near Nahua, Mont., farm
2 CONC ing there until 1947, when he returned to Hatton.   Since that time he had looked after his real estate interests here and near Finley.   He had never married.
2 CONT
2 CONT He leaves two brothers, Oliver of Hatton and Gilbert of Finley, and four sisters, Mrs. Roy (Alice) Flaten of Crookston, Minn., Mrs. Ernest (Esther) Pabst and Mrs. Alex (Annie) Jensen, both of Minneapolis, and Mrs. May Von Rueden o
2 CONC f Grand Forks. Three sisters and three brothers preceded him in death. – Hatton Free Press, Feb. 25, 1954
