1 NOTE {geni:about_me} === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT Carl Pladson Rites Friday
2 CONT
2 CONT Funeral services will be held Friday afternoon, April 6 at 2 o’clock in Goose River Church, for Carl H. Pladson, who passed away Tuesday morning  [April 3] at the Northwood Hospital.   Rev. A. E. Setness will officiate.
2 CONT Pallbearers will be J. L. Thompson, W. L. Thompson, G. O. Thompson, H. C. Huset, Carl Gulson and George Mehus. Burial will be in the family plot in the churchyard cemetery.
2 CONT Carl Henry Pladson was born near Hatton, February 14, 1890 of parents, Mr. and Mrs. Peter K. Pladson, pioneer residents of this community.   He spent his early years of his life in this community, attending grade school in Newburg
2 CONC h.   He also attended the Bruflat Academy at Portland and graduated from Concordia College, Moorhead, Minn. in 1908.
2 CONT He was employed in Fargo for several years and then moved to Fort Gary, near Winnipeg, Manitoba, Canada, where he farmed until about a year ago, when his health failed.   Before entering the Northwood Hospital he had spent about t
2 CONC wo months at the Old People’s Home there.
2 CONT In 1918 he was married to Mabel Falk at Oshkosh, Wisconsin.
2 CONT Survivors include one daughter, Betty Ann of New York City; four sisters, Mrs. Alice Flaten of Crookston, Minn., Mrs. Esther Pabst of St. Paul, Minn., Mrs. Alexander Jenson of Minneapolis, Minn., and Mrs. Mae Von Rueden of Grand F
2 CONC orks; and four brothers, Alfred and Oliver Pladson of Hatton, Edgar Pladson of Minneapolis, and Gilbert Pladson of Kensington, Minn. One daughter preceded him in death. – Hatton Free Press, April 5, 1951
