1 SUBM @S6000000003887373302@
1 NOTE OLE HENDRIKSEN
2 CONT
2 CONT Page 346-47-O. S. Johnson
2 CONT
2 CONT Ole Hendriksen was born at Nes, Hallingdal, Norway in 1839.  His parents were Hendrik
2 CONT Brunsvold and his wife Ingeborg, born Hova.  His father died when Ole was eight weeks
2 CONT old and his mother died in 1855.  In 1857 he emigrated to America and came to Rock
2 CONT Prairie, WI where he lived for two years and worked out as a farmhand. He was paid 75
2 CONT cents per day for haying and a dollar a day during harvest.  In 1859 he came to Spring
2 CONT Grove where he also worked some on the farm and also clerked at local stores.  He lived
2 CONT at Spring Grove at the time of the Civil War and at the time that Abraham Lincoln was
2 CONT elected to the presidency of the U.S.  Also while he was here at Spring Grove he was part
2 CONT of the Indian scare where the settlers didn’t know which way to go when they heard that
2 CONT the Indians were murdering and burning settlers and their homes several miles west of
2 CONT Spring Grove.  At 12:00 at night, Hendrik Tangen, who was married to his sister, came
2 CONT knocking on his door with the news that he had to take flight as fast as he could as the
2 CONT Indians were just five miles away.  He then contacted his nearest neighbor, and told him
2 CONT the news and thus the tale went from neighbor to neighbor and each family left in terror.
2 CONT They left behind all their possessions, turned their animals loose and one party even forgot
2 CONT to blow out the lamp.
2 CONT
2 CONT The two Hallingens, Ole Hendricksen & Ole Sagedalen decided that they would fight the
2 CONT Indians and rode to Spring Grove to find out if the fiends were around, but they were very
2 CONT nervous riding as they were sure that the Indians were hiding in the woods and would
2 CONT jump out and capture them.  At that time Spring Grove had a church built out of rock and
2 CONT they had decided that would make a good place from which to do battle with the Indians
2 CONT as they could fortify themselves with food, water and other provisions to fight from
2 CONT there.  At noon the next day the information reached them that the Indians were far to the
2 CONT west and what had looked like tepees where only grain stacks in the distance.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1866, Ole Hendricksen moved to Fillmore County, Minnesota where he began a
2 CONT business and became the postmaster.  He also named the settlement, Bratsberg as there
2 CONT were so many of the settlers from the Bratsberg area of Norway.  He lived here until 1879
2 CONT when he moved to Eddy County, N. D. and took himself a homestead, tree claim and
2 CONT pre-emption rights on three quarters of land where he has lived since.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married Aase Evansdtr Pladsen.  Her parents were Even Svendsen Pladsen and his wife
2 CONT Margit Ankerpladsen.  They both were from Nes, Hallingdal.  Aase died in 1888 at 44
2 CONT years of age.  Their children were five boys and three girls.  Three sons, Henrik, Selius and
2 CONT Owen are farmers near Sheyenne.  Daughter, Margit married Olav Rue and is a farmer
2 CONT near Sheyenne.  Karoline married Korleus Daltorp and farm in Montana. The third
2 CONT daughter, Ingeborg, is home with her father.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ole Hendrickson has been a County Commissioner for one term and County Treasurer for
2 CONT two terms. He was the first postmaster in the district where he lived. The post office laid
2 CONT on his land and was a simple sod house.  It was called Gedds, P.O. He had to carry the
2 CONT mail himself from Tomlingen post office which was a little ways from where New
2 CONT Rockford is situated today.  There was no train and neither New Rockford or Sheyenne
2 CONT had come into existance yet.
